<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup Label="Project">
    <!--
      Torii Nova: net10.0 (was net8.0 on master / Torii stable). Bumped here
      so JIT improvements (DynamicPGO, tiered compilation tweaks, SIMD path
      improvements) and lower-allocation GC behaviour land for Nova users.
      Direct relevance: in-gameplay Gen2 GC pauses (the dominant cause of
      mid-song hiccups in the prod data) get cheaper on net10.0.
      Android is already on net10.0-android; this brings Desktop in line.
    -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <!--
      Rebrand (May 2026): assembly + product strings dropped the "osu!" prefix
      after upstream (peppy) confirmed forks should NOT distribute binaries
      using the osu! name. The codebase is still a fork of osu-lazer, but the
      distributable carries the Torii Nova identity exclusively.

      Old → New:
        AssemblyName   "osu-torii"    → "torii"            (binary becomes torii.exe)
        AssemblyTitle  "osu!(torii)"  → "Torii Nova"
        Title          "osu! Torii"   → "Torii Nova"
        Product        "osu!(torii)"  → "Torii Nova"
        CFBundle*      "osu! Torii"   → "Torii Nova"
        CFBundleExe    "osu-torii"    → "torii"

      Warning for users: taskbar pins / desktop shortcuts pointing at the old
      osu-torii.exe path (under %LocalAppData%\torii-osu\) will break after
      first launch on the new build. Release notes need to call this out and
      tell users to re-pin from the Start menu / new shortcut.
    -->
    <Description>A free-to-win rhythm game. Rhythm is just a *click* away! (Torii Nova Version)</Description>
    <AssemblyName>torii</AssemblyName>
    <AssemblyTitle>Torii Nova</AssemblyTitle>
    <Title>Torii Nova</Title>
    <Product>Torii Nova</Product>
    <!--
      Icon: torii.ico is generated from torii-lazer-web's torii-512.png
      master (the gradient torii-gate logo used in the frontend). Multi-res
      embedded (16/24/32/48/64/128/256) so Explorer, taskbar, Alt-Tab,
      file dialogs, etc. all pick a clean rendering. The old lazer.ico is
      kept on disk but no longer referenced — beatmap.ico still uses the
      stock one because it's the file-association icon for .osz beatmap
      bundles, separate from the app icon.
    -->
    <ApplicationIcon>torii.ico</ApplicationIcon>
    <Version>0.0.0</Version>
    <FileVersion>0.0.0</FileVersion>
    <!-- Support multiple runtime identifiers for cross-platform builds -->
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64;linux-x64;osx-x64;osx-arm64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
    <!--
      ===========================================================================
      NOVA A/B EXPERIMENT (2026-06): ServerGarbageCollection flipped true -> false.
      Hiccup-report data showed Server GC was letting the managed heap balloon to
      2-3 GB+ (one session hit 6.7 GB), and the eventual blocking Gen2 then froze
      the game for 5-35 s - concentrated in SoloSongSelect, not the gameplay
      transitions this config was originally tuned for. Workstation + Concurrent
      (what upstream osu! ships) keeps a single smaller heap and collects more
      eagerly with background Gen2, trading the retry-allocation smoothness noted
      below for far shorter worst-case pauses. Shipped to Nova only, to measure
      the before/after in the hiccup dashboard. If song-select freezes + peak
      memory drop without a meaningful gameplay-transition regression, promote to
      master; otherwise revert to ServerGarbageCollection=true.
      ===========================================================================

      Server GC + Concurrent GC: workstation GC (the default) does generation 0/1
      collections on the calling thread, so the load thread takes the hit when
      a new Player instance allocates everything for the next attempt — that
      shows up as the orange GC spikes in the frame statistics overlay on retry.
      Server GC runs collections on dedicated background threads per logical
      core, keeping pauses off the audio/update threads. Standard tuning for
      rhythm-game-class realtime workloads on .NET.

      RetainVM: keep virtual memory mapped after a Gen2 collection instead of
      decommitting pages back to the OS. Live hiccup-report data showed the
      worst post-gameplay stutters (Player → SoloResultsScreen transition)
      were Gen2 cascades with +gen0=46 +gen1=18 +gen2=9 in a single frame —
      the runtime panic-collected because the working set dropped from ~500MB
      to ~250MB the moment HitObjects / replay frames / audio buffers were
      released. Retaining the VM eliminates the decommit trigger; next
      gameplay round reuses the warm pages instead of re-committing them.
      Trade-off: idle memory usage stays higher between gameplay rounds.
      Worth it for the frame-time win at end of every map.

      DynamicAdaptationMode=0: opt out of DATAS (Dynamic Adaptation To
      Application Sizes), enabled by default on .NET 9+/10 (Nova channel).
      DATAS aggressively right-sizes the GC heap to minimise idle memory,
      which is the OPPOSITE of what a realtime game wants — we'd rather
      pay a stable memory cost than have heap reorganisations show up as
      stutter at gameplay transition boundaries. No-op on .NET 8 (the
      stable Torii channel) where DATAS isn't default; pre-emptively set
      so the property survives any future bump to .NET 10+ on master.
    -->
    <ServerGarbageCollection>false</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>true</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
    <RetainVMGarbageCollection>true</RetainVMGarbageCollection>
    <!--
      TORII_NOVA: compile-time marker for the experimental stream. Source
      that should only run on Nova builds gates itself with `#if TORII_NOVA`.
      Master / Torii-stable does NOT define this constant, so the same
      source file produces a no-op gate there. Currently used to flip the
      renderer default to Deferred for first-Nova-run users — see
      OsuGameDesktop.applyNovaRendererDefault().
    -->
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);TORII_NOVA</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup Label="Runtime host configuration">
    <!-- See <RetainVMGarbageCollection> above for the GC tuning rationale.
         DynamicAdaptationMode doesn't have a dedicated MSBuild property, so
         it goes through RuntimeHostConfigurationOption which flows the
         same way into runtimeconfig.json. -->
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.GC.DynamicAdaptationMode" Value="0" />
    <!--
      NoAffinitize: Server GC by default pins its heap-collector threads to
      the first N logical CPUs (one per heap). On a gaming machine the OS
      scheduler tends to put high-priority threads (audio, input, our 2000hz
      update loop) on those same low-numbered cores, so GC pauses end up
      fighting the hot threads for the same hardware. Letting the GC
      threads float means the kernel picks idle cores for collection work
      and our hot loop keeps its current core. Recommended for realtime
      desktop workloads on Server GC (containers / fixed-affinity hosts
      should leave this default).
    -->
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.GC.NoAffinitize" Value="true" />
    <!--
      LOHThreshold: bump from default 85000 bytes to 120000. The default
      cutoff puts audio buffers (BASS ~64KB-100KB chunks) and replay-frame
      blocks (~80-110KB) into the Large Object Heap, which only compacts
      during Gen2. Raising the threshold keeps those medium-lifetime
      allocations in the small-object heap, where they're collected
      cheaply in Gen0/Gen1 instead of pooling into Gen2 pressure.
      120000 is conservative — covers the audio + replay sizes we
      actually see in hiccup-report data without disturbing genuinely
      large allocations (skin textures, score serialisation buffers etc.
      are all well above 120KB).
    -->
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.GC.LOHThreshold" Value="120000" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <StartupObject>osu.Desktop.Program</StartupObject>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup Label="Project References">
    <ProjectReference Include="..\osu.Game.Tournament\osu.Game.Tournament.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\osu.Game\osu.Game.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\osu.Game.Rulesets.Osu\osu.Game.Rulesets.Osu.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\osu.Game.Rulesets.Catch\osu.Game.Rulesets.Catch.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\osu.Game.Rulesets.Mania\osu.Game.Rulesets.Mania.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\osu.Game.Rulesets.Taiko\osu.Game.Rulesets.Taiko.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup Label="Package References">
    <PackageReference Include="System.IO.Packaging" Version="9.0.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="DiscordRichPresence" Version="1.2.1.24" />
    <PackageReference Include="Velopack" Version="0.0.1298" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup Label="Resources">
    <!-- Embed BOTH icons: torii.ico is the active app icon (used at runtime
         + by the .exe metadata), lazer.ico is kept as an embedded resource
         for backward compatibility with anything that references it by
         resource name. Once nothing references lazer.ico anymore (audit
         again in a release or two), remove this. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="torii.ico" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="lazer.ico" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup Label="Windows Icons" Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('$(RuntimeIdentifier)').StartsWith('win'))">
    <Content Include="*.ico" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- macOS specific configurations -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('OSX'))">
    <CFBundleName>Torii Nova</CFBundleName>
    <CFBundleDisplayName>Torii Nova</CFBundleDisplayName>
    <CFBundleIdentifier>com.shikkesora.torii</CFBundleIdentifier>
    <CFBundleVersion>0.0.0</CFBundleVersion>
    <CFBundlePackageType>APPL</CFBundlePackageType>
    <CFBundleSignature>????</CFBundleSignature>
    <CFBundleExecutable>torii</CFBundleExecutable>
    <CFBundleIconFile>torii.icns</CFBundleIconFile>
    <NSPrincipalClass>NSApplication</NSPrincipalClass>
    <NSHighResolutionCapable>true</NSHighResolutionCapable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="macOS Icons" Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('OSX'))">
    <Content Include="torii.icns" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" Condition="Exists('torii.icns')" />
    <Content Include="lazer.icns" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" Condition="Exists('lazer.icns')" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Folder Include="LowLatency\AMD\" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
